.TH PV 1 "" "pv 0.6.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
pv \- monitor and manage data flowing through a pipe
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pv
[OPTIONS] [FILES]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
Copy input files in order to standard output and report progress on standard error.
A missing input list or a filename of \- reads standard input.
Use \-o to write a destination file. Progress is hidden when stderr is not a terminal,
unless \-f or \-n is selected. Binary data is passed through unchanged.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-size <SIZE>
Estimated total bytes or lines; accepts decimal K/M/G/T suffixes or @PATH
.TP
.B \-t, \-\-timer
Show elapsed time
.TP
.B \-w, \-\-width <WIDTH>
Display width; 0 selects terminal detection
.TP
.B \-H, \-\-height <HEIGHT>
Terminal rows for cursor positioning; 0 selects detection
.TP
.B \-b, \-\-bytes
Show transferred byte or line count
.TP
.B \-r, \-\-rate
Show current transfer rate
.TP
.B \-a, \-\-average\-rate
Show rolling average transfer rate
.TP
.B \-e, \-\-eta
Show estimated time remaining
.TP
.B \-I, \-\-fineta
Show estimated local completion time
.TP
.B \-l, \-\-line\-mode
Count newline\-terminated records instead of bytes
.TP
.B \-0, \-\-null
Count NUL\-terminated records; implies \-\-line\-mode
.TP
.B \-E, \-\-skip\-errors...
Skip seekable input errors with zero padding; repeat to reduce diagnostics
.TP
.B \-Z, \-\-error\-skip\-block <ERROR_SKIP_BLOCK>
On read errors, advance to the next BYTES block boundary
.TP
.B \-O, \-\-sparse
Seek over zero blocks when writing a regular file
.TP
.B \-i, \-\-interval <INTERVAL>
Seconds between display updates; 0 selects 0.1 [default: 1]
.TP
.B \-m, \-\-average\-rate\-window <AVERAGE_RATE_WINDOW>
Rolling average window in seconds; 0 selects 30 [default: 30]
.TP
.B \-N, \-\-name <NAME>
Prefix the display with NAME
.TP
.B \-T, \-\-buffer\-percent
Show transfer buffer occupancy; implies \-\-no\-splice
.TP
.B \-A, \-\-last\-written <NUM>
Show the last NUM bytes; implies \-\-no\-splice
.TP
.B \-B, \-\-buffer\-size <BYTES>
Transfer buffer size (K/M/G/T suffixes); implies \-\-no\-splice
.TP
.B \-C, \-\-no\-splice
Use buffered read/write rather than kernel\-assisted copying
.TP
.B \-J, \-\-pipe\-buffer\-size <BYTES>
Best\-effort Linux pipe capacity in bytes
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quiet
Suppress progress output (explicit \-\-stats still prints a summary)
.TP
.B \-p, \-\-progress
Show progress bar
.TP
.B \-F, \-\-format <FORMAT>
Custom display or numeric format string
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-numeric
Write raw numeric progress records to standard error
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-stats
Print minimum, average, maximum and deviation of byte rates
.TP
.B \-L, \-\-rate\-limit <RATE>
Maximum bytes or lines per second; 0 disables throttling
.TP
.B \-o, \-\-output <FILE>
Write data to FILE instead of stdout
.TP
.B \-f, \-\-force
Show progress even when stderr is not a terminal
.TP
.B \-k, \-\-si
Use base\-1000 units and interpret following quantity suffixes as SI
.TP
.B \-8, \-\-bits
Display byte counts and rates as bits
.TP
.B \-S, \-\-stop\-at\-size
Stop at the size selected by \-\-size without consuming subsequent input
.TP
.B \-W, \-\-wait
Start reporting and timing after the first byte is transferred
.TP
.B \-D, \-\-delay\-start <DELAY_START>
Delay only reporting by SEC seconds; never delay data transfer
.TP
.B \-g, \-\-gauge
For unknown sizes, display rate relative to the maximum observed rate
.TP
.B \-u, \-\-bar\-style <BAR_STYLE>
Select progress bar appearance [default: plain] [possible values: plain, block, granular, shaded]
.TP
.B \-X, \-\-discard
Consume and count input without emitting data
.TP
.B \-Y, \-\-sync
Synchronize regular\-file output after each write
.TP
.B \-K, \-\-direct\-io
Use aligned direct I/O for Linux regular files; preserve short tails
.TP
.B \-U, \-\-store\-and\-forward <FILE>
Stage all input in FILE before forwarding; \- selects a temporary file
.TP
.B \-P, \-\-pidfile <FILE>
Write this process ID to FILE
.TP
.B \-c, \-\-cursor
Coordinate terminal rows between Rust pv instances (Unix)
.TP
.B \-x, \-\-extra\-display <SPEC>
Also report to window/process titles, optionally with :FORMAT
.TP
.B \-9, \-\-conemu
Set terminal progress state using OSC 9;4
.TP
.B \-d, \-\-watchfd <PID[:FD]|=NAME|@LISTFILE>...
Observe byte offsets in another process using Linux /proc
.TP
.B \-R, \-\-remote <PID>
Change explicit settings of a running Rust pv (Unix)
.TP
.B \-Q, \-\-query <PID>
Report a running Rust pv snapshot (Unix)
.TP
.B \-M, \-\-monitor <SIDE>
Run \-\- COMMAND ARGS and monitor its input, output or both (Unix) [possible values: in, 0, out, 1, both, 2]
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Print version information.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print help
.SH QUANTITIES
Sizes, rates and buffer sizes accept decimal numbers with K, M, G or T suffixes
(case insensitive). Suffixes use powers of 1024; \-k selects powers of 1000
for subsequent quantity options. In line mode, size and rate count records.
A size of @PATH uses the size of that path, recursively for directories.
The transfer buffer defaults to 128 KiB and is limited to 64 MiB.
.SH FORMATS
Use \-F FORMAT to choose display fields. Supported fields include:
.TP
.B %b, %t, %r, %a
Transferred count, elapsed time, current rate and rolling average rate.
.TP
.B %p, %e, %I, %N
Progress bar, remaining time, local completion time and name.
.TP
.B %T, %nA, %L
Buffer utilization (or kernel copy method), last n written bytes and previous line.
.TP
.B %%
A literal percent sign.
.PP
Numeric mode emits raw records to stderr. With \-n \-b it emits byte or line counts;
without a count selection it normally emits percentage. Explicit \-F selects its fields.
Unknown-size progress cannot provide a meaningful percentage or ETA.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B pv archive.tar | gzip > archive.tar.gz
Compress a file while displaying progress.
.TP
.B pv \-L 10M image.iso > copy.iso
Limit the transfer to 10 MiB per second.
.TP
.B producer | pv \-n \-b > output.bin 2> counts.txt
Capture data separately from numeric byte counts.
.TP
.B pv \-s 100 \-S input.bin > first.bin
Copy at most 100 bytes, leaving subsequent input unconsumed.
.TP
.B pv \-l \-s 100 records.txt > copy.txt
Count newline-terminated records using an explicit total.
.TP
.B pv \-f \-F '%N %b %r %e' \-N backup input.bin > output.bin
Force a named custom progress display.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero means success. Nonzero statuses identify failures; relevant upstream-compatible
bits are 2 for opening/accessing files, 8 for input/read failures, 16 for output/write
failures, and 64 for invalid options. Other failures may return 1. Monitor mode
propagates the monitored command's exit status. Inspect stderr for details.
.SH ENVIRONMENT AND FILES
There is no configuration file or PV_* option-default environment interface.
The standard temporary directory is used for temporary store-and-forward files
and private Unix IPC sockets. The IPC directory must be private and owned by the
current user; ordinary copying remains possible when IPC is unavailable.
TZ affects local completion-time formatting. Terminal capabilities affect display.
A named store-and-forward file and a requested PID file remain on disk after exit.
.SH COMPATIBILITY
This is the Rust implementation, not the original Pipe Viewer. Upstream pv 1.12.0
is the tested behavioral reference. Remote/query use a private protocol between
Rust pv instances and cannot control upstream pv. Descriptor watching requires
Linux /proc. Direct I/O and kernel copy acceleration are Linux-specific; monitor,
cursor coordination and remote/query require Unix. Ordinary copying is portable.
Display styling and rate statistics are not guaranteed to match upstream exactly.
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
If no progress is visible, ensure stderr goes to a terminal or use \-f.
Use \-s to provide a known total for a pipe. A broken pipe means the downstream
reader stopped; check its exit status. For input/output failures, check the named
paths, permissions and available destination space. \-E is intended for recovery
from seekable input errors and may replace unreadable ranges with zeros.
Do not use it to hide failures in a normal backup. Use \-C to compare buffered
copy behavior when investigating kernel-path differences.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cat (1),
.BR dd (1),
.BR gzip (1)
.PP
